<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter13_inheritance_base_and_derived_classes">
    <title>Inheritance: Base and Derived Classes</title>

    <p>
        <term>Inheritance</term> allows one class to begin with the members of
        another class and then add or replace behavior. The existing class is the
        <term>base class</term>, and the new class is the <term>derived class</term>.
        Public inheritance expresses an is-a relationship.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter13_subsection_inheritance_syntax">
        <title>Basic Inheritance Syntax</title>

        <p>
            The syntax places the base class after a colon in the derived-class
            declaration:
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter13_basic_inheritance">
            <title>A derived class inherits a public member</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter13_basic_inheritance"
                     language="cpp" interactive="yes" line-numbers="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class Employee {
public:
    void report_role() const {
        std::cout << "Employee\n";
    }
};

class HourlyEmployee : public Employee {
public:
    void report_pay_type() const {
        std::cout << "Paid by the hour\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    HourlyEmployee employee;

    employee.report_role();
    employee.report_pay_type();

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            The public member function <c>report_role()</c> belongs to
            <c>Employee</c>, but an <c>HourlyEmployee</c> object can call it because
            it inherits that public member. The derived class also contributes its
            own <c>report_pay_type()</c> member function.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter13_subsection_base_and_derived_members">
        <title>Base and Derived Members</title>

        <p>
            A derived class does not receive unrestricted access to every base-class
            member. Access control still applies:
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table_chapter13_inheritance_access">
            <title>Access to base-class members</title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <col width="24%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="26%"/>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell>Base member</cell>
                    <cell>Base class</cell>
                    <cell>Derived class</cell>
                    <cell>Unrelated code</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><c>public</c></cell>
                    <cell>Accessible</cell>
                    <cell>Accessible</cell>
                    <cell>Accessible through the public interface</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><c>protected</c></cell>
                    <cell>Accessible</cell>
                    <cell>Accessible</cell>
                    <cell>Not directly accessible</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><c>private</c></cell>
                    <cell>Accessible</cell>
                    <cell>Not directly accessible</cell>
                    <cell>Not directly accessible</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            A <c>protected</c> member is available to the class that declares it and
            to derived classes. It is not available to ordinary client code. This
            can be useful, but protected data also creates tighter coupling between
            a base class and its derived classes. Private data with public or
            protected member functions often provides better control.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter13_protected_member">
            <title>A derived class accesses a protected member</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter13_protected_member"
                     language="cpp" interactive="yes" line-numbers="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

class Employee {
protected:
    std::string name;

public:
    Employee(const std::string& employee_name)
        : name{employee_name} {
    }
};

class HourlyEmployee : public Employee {
public:
    HourlyEmployee(const std::string& employee_name)
        : Employee{employee_name} {
    }

    void report_name() const {
        std::cout << name << '\n';
    }
};

int main() {
    HourlyEmployee employee{"Amina"};
    employee.report_name();

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            The derived class can use <c>name</c> because it is protected. However,
            code in <c>main()</c> cannot access <c>employee.name</c> directly.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter13_subsection_constructor_order">
        <title>Construction Order</title>

        <p>
            When a derived object is created, the base-class portion is constructed
            first. The derived-class portion is constructed afterward. This order
            ensures that the base part exists before the derived constructor uses
            it. Destruction occurs in the reverse order; the derived part is
            destroyed before the base part.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter13_constructor_order">
            <title>Construction and destruction in an inherited class</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter13_constructor_order"
                     language="cpp" interactive="yes" line-numbers="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class Employee {
public:
    Employee() {
        std::cout << "Employee constructed\n";
    }

    ~Employee() {
        std::cout << "Employee destroyed\n";
    }
};

class HourlyEmployee : public Employee {
public:
    HourlyEmployee() {
        std::cout << "HourlyEmployee constructed\n";
    }

    ~HourlyEmployee() {
        std::cout << "HourlyEmployee destroyed\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    HourlyEmployee employee;
    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            The output shows that construction proceeds from base to derived, while
            destruction proceeds from derived to base.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter13_subsection_inheritance_visualization">
        <title>Visualizing a Base and Derived Object</title>

        <p>
            The following visualization shows a derived object being created from a
            base class and then calling members from both classes.
        </p>

        <interactive xml:id="interactive_chapter13_inheritance_base_derived"
                     iframe="https://pythontutor.com/iframe-embed.html#code=%23include%20%3Ciostream%3E%0A%0Aclass%20Employee%20%7B%0Apublic%3A%0A%20%20%20%20Employee%28%29%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20%22Employee%20constructed%5Cn%22%3B%0A%20%20%20%20%7D%0A%0A%20%20%20%20void%20report_role%28%29%20const%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20%22Employee%5Cn%22%3B%0A%20%20%20%20%7D%0A%7D%3B%0A%0Aclass%20HourlyEmployee%20%3A%20public%20Employee%20%7B%0Apublic%3A%0A%20%20%20%20void%20report_pay_type%28%29%20const%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20%22Paid%20by%20the%20hour%5Cn%22%3B%0A%20%20%20%20%7D%0A%7D%3B%0A%0Aint%20main%28%29%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20HourlyEmployee%20employee%3B%0A%20%20%20%20employee.report_role%28%29%3B%0A%20%20%20%20employee.report_pay_type%28%29%3B%0A%20%20%20%20return%200%3B%0A%7D&amp;codeDivHeight=380&amp;codeDivWidth=420&amp;cppShowMemAddrs=true&amp;curInstr=0&amp;origin=opt-frontend.js&amp;py=cpp_g%2B%2B9.3.0"
                     width="100%"
                     aspect="16:10">
        </interactive>
    </subsection>

    <exercises xml:id="exercises_chapter13_inheritance_base_and_derived">
        <title>Exercises</title>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_inheritance_1">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In <c>class HourlyEmployee : public Employee</c>, which class is
                    the base class?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <choices randomize="yes">
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p><c>Employee</c></p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>The class after <c>public</c> is the base class.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p><c>HourlyEmployee</c></p></statement>
                    <feedback><p><c>HourlyEmployee</c> is the derived class.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p><c>public</c></p></statement>
                    <feedback><p><c>public</c> specifies the inheritance access mode.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p><c>employee</c></p></statement>
                    <feedback><p><c>employee</c> is an object, not a class.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_inheritance_2">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Which member can the derived class access directly but ordinary
                    code in <c>main()</c> cannot access directly?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <choices randomize="yes">
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>A public member</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Public members are available through the public interface.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p>A protected member</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Protected members are accessible to derived classes but not ordinary client code.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>A private member declared in the base class</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Private base members are not directly accessible in derived classes.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>A local variable in <c>main()</c></p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Inheritance does not provide access to local variables.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_inheritance_multiple">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Which statements about the inheritance example are correct?
                    Select all that apply.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <choices randomize="yes" multiple-correct="yes">
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p><c>HourlyEmployee</c> can use public members inherited from <c>Employee</c>.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Public inheritance preserves access to public base members.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p><c>HourlyEmployee</c> can directly access every private member of <c>Employee</c>.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Private base members are not directly accessible in derived classes.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p>The base part is constructed before the derived part.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>The base must be initialized before the derived portion.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p>The derived part is destroyed before the base part.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Destruction occurs in the reverse of construction order.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_inheritance_3">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Place these events in their construction order: construction of
                    the derived part, construction of the base part, construction of
                    the derived object's members.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    The base part is constructed first, followed by the derived
                    object's members, and then the derived constructor body runs.
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_inheritance_parsons"
                  number="yes"
                  adaptive="yes"
                  indentation="show">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Arrange the blocks to define <c>HourlyEmployee</c> as a public
                    derived class of <c>Employee</c>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <blocks randomize="yes">
                <block order="1">
                    <cline>class HourlyEmployee : public Employee {</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="2">
                    <cline>public:</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="3">
                    <cline>    void report_pay_type() const {</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="4">
                    <cline>        std::cout &lt;&lt; "Paid by the hour\n";</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="5">
                    <cline>    }</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="6">
                    <cline>};</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="1" correct="no">
                    <cline>class HourlyEmployee : private Employee {</cline>
                </block>
            </blocks>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>
</section>
